# SyncSpace Technical Specification

This is the project's architecture and product source of truth. It describes planned behavior explicitly; `process.md` records implementation status. Update this file with every architecture-level change.

## 1. Project Overview

SyncSpace is a multi-user workspace, project, board, and task application. Phases 0–10 are implemented locally; later roadmap features and production validation remain outstanding.

## 2. Product Vision

Users create workspaces, invite colleagues, organize work on boards, and see authorized changes in near real time. The goal is a credible, maintainable collaboration product that demonstrates real engineering tradeoffs.

## 3. Core Product Requirements

Hierarchy: User → Workspace → Project → Board → Column → Task. Membership, invitations, project/board/task collaboration, assignees, labels, comments, mentions, activity feeds, core notifications and due reminders, scoped task search, authenticated sockets, presence, optimistic UI, and conflict detection are implemented locally. Invitation email delivery, attachments, watchers, and production operations remain planned. MVP is the two-user journey defined in section 38.

## 4. Non-Goals

No microservices, Kafka, hand-built CRDT, permanent presence records, or large analytics system in the MVP. Mobile native apps are outside the initial scope.

## 5. Mandatory Engineering Rules

Read `AGENTS.md`, this file, and `process.md` before changing code. Every meaningful commit updates `process.md`; architecture changes update this file. Never store secrets, trust client-supplied identity/role, skip server authorization, or claim tests passed without running them. UTC storage, UUID resource IDs, migrations for schema changes, and explicit documentation of debt are required.

## 6. Technology Stack

pnpm monorepo; Next.js App Router, React, strict TypeScript, Tailwind CSS; NestJS API; PostgreSQL with Prisma migrations; Redis; Supabase Auth with `@supabase/ssr` and JOSE JWT verification; Socket.IO for board events. Dependencies are pinned in the lockfile. TanStack Query, React Hook Form, and dnd-kit enter when their features are implemented.

## 7. System Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
 B[Browser] --> W[Next.js]
 W -->|REST authoritative| A[NestJS]
 W -. Socket.IO events .-> A
 A --> P[(PostgreSQL)]
 A --> R[(Redis)]
```

The browser sends resource mutations through REST. The API validates identity, membership, input, and version, commits the database transaction, then publishes a small event. Clients reconcile against canonical REST data. Socket delivery is best effort; reconnects reauthenticate and refetch. Phase 1 provides only the health path and connectivity checks.

## 8. Repository Structure

`apps/web` Next.js, Supabase SSR helpers, and workspace/project/board/task pages; `apps/api` NestJS, Prisma, auth, users, workspaces, and projects; `packages/shared` public contracts when introduced; `scripts` governance; `docs` supplemental reviews/operations notes; root config and governance files. Do not share Prisma models with browser code.

## 9. Frontend Architecture

Use App Router and server rendering where practical. Auth pages use a Supabase browser client; protected `/app` pages use a server client with cookie sessions, `getClaims()` verification, and a bearer token to fetch canonical API data. Middleware refreshes cookies and forwards private cache headers. Invite continuation is restricted to `/invite/<token>` and stored briefly in an HttpOnly cookie through OAuth. Client components handle interactive forms and sign-out. Phase 3 pages list/create workspaces, manage members/settings, show a one-time invitation link, and accept invitations. Phase 4 pages list/create projects and boards, edit project settings, and add/rename/reorder/delete columns with version conflict feedback. Board task state uses local optimistic movement and server refresh after Socket.IO events; TanStack Query may replace this cache strategy later. Task drag/drop rolls back on failure and provides keyboard arrow movement. Theme modes: light, dark, system, persisted per user in a later phase.

## 10. Backend Architecture

NestJS modules by domain: Auth, Users, Workspaces, Invitations, Projects, Boards, Tasks, Comments, Realtime, Presence, Notifications, Activity, Files, Search. Controllers handle transport, services hold rules, Prisma repositories persist. Implemented: PrismaModule, HealthModule, AuthModule, UsersModule, WorkspacesModule, ProjectsModule (including boards and columns), and TasksModule (including labels and assignments). Health checks PostgreSQL and Redis. Shared bootstrap validation rejects missing foundation environment variables. Auth can return 503 when Supabase is not configured in local development.

## 11. Database Architecture

PostgreSQL is durable truth. Prisma schema and checked-in migrations define structure. Foreign keys, unique constraints, transactional writes, and indexed membership/path lookups are required. Initial migration introduced an infrastructure probe table; Phase 2 added `users` with unique `auth_subject`; Phase 3 added `workspaces`, `workspace_members`, and `workspace_invitations` with foreign keys, unique membership, and hashed invitation tokens. Phase 4 added `projects`, `boards`, and `columns` with lineage foreign keys and order indexes. Phase 5 added `tasks`, `task_assignees`, `labels`, and `task_labels`, with a composite task-column/board foreign key and rank uniqueness. Phase 9 added `comments`, `comment_mentions`, and mention `notifications`. Phase 10 added `activity_events` with workspace/task indexes. Phase 11 expands notification types, permits workspace-only and system records, adds a unique reminder key, and indexes task due dates. Later domain tables arrive with their phases. Never edit production schema manually.

## 12. Data Models

Implemented: `User(id, authSubject, email, displayName, createdAt, updatedAt)` with unique Supabase subject; `Workspace(id, name, ownerId, archivedAt)`; `WorkspaceMember(id, workspaceId, userId, role, joinedAt)` with unique workspace/user; `WorkspaceInvitation(id, workspaceId, email, role, tokenHash, expiresAt, acceptedAt, revokedAt, invitedBy)`. Implemented Phase 4: `Project(id, workspaceId, name, description, status, createdBy, archivedAt)`, `Board(id, projectId, name, version)`, and `Column(id, boardId, name, position)`. Implemented Phase 5: `Task(id, boardId, columnId, title, description, rank, priority, dueAt, createdBy, version, completedAt, archivedAt)`, `TaskAssignee(taskId, userId)`, `Label(id, projectId, name, color)`, and `TaskLabel(taskId, labelId)`. Implemented Phase 9: `Comment(id, taskId, authorId, content, version, deletedAt)`, `CommentMention(commentId, userId)`, and `Notification(id, workspaceId, taskId?, commentId?, recipientId, actorId?, type, readAt, dedupeKey?)`; Phase 11 supports `MENTION`, `ASSIGNMENT`, `COMMENT`, `ROLE_CHANGED`, and `DUE_SOON`. Phase 10 adds `ActivityEvent(id, workspaceId, projectId?, taskId?, actorId, entityType, entityId, eventType, metadata, createdAt)`. All IDs UUID and timestamps UTC. Planned: `Attachment` and `TaskWatcher`.

## 13. Authentication Architecture

Phase 2 implementation: Supabase Auth email/password and Google OAuth via PKCE callback. SSR stores session in cookies. Next.js `getClaims()` verifies before rendering `/app`; middleware refreshes the session and returns cache headers. The browser/server forwards the access token to NestJS. API independently verifies ES256/RS256 JWT signature against the configured project's JWKS, issuer `${SUPABASE_URL}/auth/v1`, audience `authenticated`, expiry, UUID subject, role, email, and non-anonymous claim. The verified subject maps to a local `User`, created on first protected profile access. Neither cookie content nor client-supplied IDs authorize the API. Socket handshakes reuse this verification and disconnect at JWT expiry. The provider URL/publishable key and Google provider must be configured externally; no service role key is used.

## 14. Authorization / RBAC

Roles: OWNER (settings, membership roles, transfer, archive); ADMIN (rename, invite, projects/boards/columns); EDITOR (tasks, labels, comments; later files); VIEWER (read only). Phase 3 API checks membership on each workspace path and hides inaccessible workspaces with 404. OWNER cannot be removed/demoted through the role route; transfer changes `ownerId` and both memberships in a serializable transaction. Admin cannot invite admin; no invitation can grant owner. Phase 4 project and board requests resolve workspace lineage server-side and enforce the same membership rules. Phase 5 task requests resolve board/project/workspace lineage and reject assignments to non-members and labels from other projects. Phase 9 comments inherit task lineage, permit editors to create, and restrict edits/deletes to the author; mentioned emails must resolve to current workspace members. Socket board/task joins resolve the same lineage and membership; removal disconnects that user's sockets. Future nested resources must do the same.

## 15. REST API Design

Version prefix `/api/v1`. Implemented: `GET /health`; `GET/PATCH /users/me`; `GET/POST /workspaces`, `GET/PATCH/DELETE /workspaces/:id` (DELETE archives), `GET /workspaces/:id/members`, `PATCH/DELETE /workspaces/:id/members/:memberId`, `DELETE /workspaces/:id/members/me`, `POST /workspaces/:id/transfer-ownership`, `GET/POST /workspaces/:id/invitations`, `DELETE /workspaces/:id/invitations/:invitationId`, `POST /invitations/accept`. Invitation create returns a raw token only once and uses `Cache-Control: private, no-store`; there is no email sender yet. Implemented Phase 4: `GET/POST /workspaces/:workspaceId/projects`, `GET/PATCH/DELETE /projects/:projectId`, `GET/POST /projects/:projectId/boards`, `GET /boards/:boardId`, and board column add/rename/delete/order routes. Implemented Phase 5: board task list/create, task read/edit/move/copy/archive/permanent delete, task assignee and label replacement, and project label list/create. Phase 9 adds `GET/POST /tasks/:taskId/comments` and `PATCH/DELETE /comments/:commentId`; comment lists return 50 items plus cursor. Phase 10 adds `GET /tasks/:taskId/activity` and `GET /workspaces/:workspaceId/activity`, both scoped and paginated at 50 events. Phase 11 adds `GET /notifications`, `GET /notifications/unread-count`, `PATCH /notifications/:id/read`, and `POST /notifications/read-all`. Phase 13 adds `GET /workspaces/:workspaceId/search` with required query, optional project/assignee/priority filters, and 20-result pages. Planned: file endpoints. Mutations return canonical state. Validation errors 400, unauthenticated 401, unauthorized 403, absent 404, conflicts 409.

## 16. Real-Time Architecture

The `/realtime` Socket.IO namespace uses WebSocket transport and verifies the Supabase bearer token during handshake. The server enforces `WEB_ORIGIN`, disconnects at JWT expiry, and checks workspace membership through board lineage on each `board.join`. A connection joins one `board:{id}` and `workspace:{id}` room plus its `user:{id}` room; switching boards clears previous board/workspace rooms. Membership removal and project/workspace archive disconnect affected sockets. REST mutations publish best-effort events after durable commit. The board client deduplicates event IDs and refetches on reconnect, including after token expiry. Redis tracks workspace presence by socket. There is no durable event replay/outbox or cross-instance socket adapter yet; missed publications require a page refresh or reconnection. The adapter is planned for Phase 16.

## 17. WebSocket Event Catalog

Implemented: `task.created`, `task.updated`, `task.moved`, `task.deleted`, `board.updated`, `presence.online/offline`, `comment.created/updated/deleted`, `typing.started/stopped`, and `notification.created` for mention, assignment, comment, and role recipients. Planned: `member.joined/left`. Task/board/comment envelopes contain event ID, scope IDs, actor, entity, version, timestamp, and an empty payload; clients refetch canonical REST state. Presence envelopes contain workspace, user, event ID, timestamp, and display name; `board.join` and heartbeat acknowledgements return an authorized online-member snapshot. Typing events are scoped to an authorized task room, rate limited per socket, and expire automatically after five seconds.

## 18. Presence System

Implemented Redis sorted sets per workspace/user with one 90-second lease per socket plus a workspace user index. A Lua script atomically prunes expired socket leases, renews a heartbeat, and detects the first active socket; another script removes only the disconnecting socket and detects the last. The board client heartbeats every 30 seconds; snapshots prune expired users and filter against current workspace membership. A socket join and each heartbeat check board authorization. `presence.online/offline` events update open boards; periodic snapshots recover from missed disconnect events or process crashes. Redis errors degrade only the online indicator, leaving authorized board events usable. Presence never becomes permanent PostgreSQL truth. Multi-instance event fanout remains Phase 16.

## 19. Concurrency Strategy

Boards use integer `version` for column changes. Each column mutation sends `expectedVersion`; the server claims the matching board version in a serializable transaction and returns 409 on stale state. Reordering validates the exact column set and rewrites at most 20 positions. Tasks use their own integer `version`; edits, moves, assignments, and labels check `expectedVersion` and return 409 on a mismatch. Task rank is an 18-digit zero-padded string; midpoint insertion normally changes one task. When a gap is exhausted, a serializable transaction temporarily re-ranks then evenly spaces tasks in the destination column. A unique column/rank constraint rejects collisions.

## 20. Optimistic UI Strategy

For task moves, snapshot the board task list, apply an immediate local change, send the expected version, reconcile the returned canonical task, and roll back with an understandable conflict message on rejection. A synchronous pending guard prevents duplicate move requests before React rerenders. The board refetches after REST moves, conflicts, socket events, and reconnection; socket refresh waits while an optimistic move is in flight. Task detail forms remount on server version changes and a 409 invalidates the route before rendering canonical state. Two concurrent writers are tested: one commit wins, the other receives 409. Never silently overwrite an edit.

## 21. Redis Architecture

Phase 1 connection check proves configuration. Phase 7 uses Redis for ephemeral socket presence; Phase 9 typing uses a short in-process timer. Phase 14 uses Redis fixed-window counters for invitation create/accept limits across API instances. The Socket.IO Redis adapter remains planned. PostgreSQL remains business truth. Redis outage fails readiness, makes presence unavailable, and causes invitation routes to return 503 because their abuse limits cannot be enforced; other board REST and socket events continue on a single API instance.

## 22. File Storage Architecture

Phase 12: private object bucket with signed upload/download URLs, workspace authorization, MIME/size validation, metadata in PostgreSQL, and explicit deletion handling. Never expose bucket-wide credentials.

## 23. Notification Architecture

Phase 9 stores `MENTION` notifications for current workspace members in the same transaction as a comment. Phase 11 adds a member-scoped paginated inbox, unread count, idempotent read and read-all APIs, and assignment/comment/role records created with their primary mutation. New assignments notify only added people; comment recipients exclude the author and people already mentioned. A worker scans at startup and every 15 minutes for tasks due within 24 hours or overdue by less than 24 hours. It filters active tasks, projects, workspaces, and current assignees, then uses a unique task/due-time/recipient key to prevent duplicate reminders across scans or API instances. `notification.created` is a best-effort hint for mutation-driven alerts; PostgreSQL is canonical. Invitation email delivery remains planned and needs an external email channel.

## 24. Search Architecture

Phase 13 task search uses a PostgreSQL GIN index on a `simple` full-text document of title and description, parameterized `plainto_tsquery`, and optional project, assignee, and priority filters. The API checks current workspace membership before querying, excludes archived tasks/projects/workspaces, orders by relevance and task ID, and returns 20 results per page up to page 10. The web provides the same filters and accessible task links. Add dedicated search infrastructure only with measured need.

## 25. Security Requirements

Validate all API inputs; verify auth server-side; enforce RBAC on REST and sockets; escape/render comments safely; hash invitation tokens; rate limit sensitive routes; use secure headers and least-privilege secrets. Phase 14 limits invitation creation to 20 per account/workspace/hour and token acceptance to 10 per account/five minutes with atomic Redis counters, returning 429 on excess and 503 when Redis is unavailable. Web and API set `nosniff`, frame denial, referrer, and camera/microphone/geolocation policy headers. Bound upload size and type when files arrive. Audit privileged actions. Do not leak membership through errors or events. Production CORS is restricted to `WEB_ORIGIN`.

## 26. Performance Requirements

Avoid N+1 reads, whole-board broadcasts for tiny updates, and large socket payloads. Paginate comments, activity, notifications, and search. Add indexes for foreign keys and common filters. Measure latency before optimization.

## 27. Observability

Phase 1 uses Nest's built-in console logs and health checks for PostgreSQL/Redis. Future stages add structured request logs, request IDs, error tracking, metrics, and dashboards. Logs must not include tokens or user content by default.

## 28. Testing Strategy

Unit: permissions, versioning, ranking, invitation validation, event construction, presence calculations. Integration: DB, auth, membership, REST, conflicts, socket authorization/delivery. Playwright E2E: signup→workspace→project→task; invite acceptance; two-client move; viewer rejection; concurrent conflict. Seed only explicitly fake users. Current tests validate bootstrap/health, governance, JWT rejection/acceptance using local signed tokens and JWKS, profile persistence, workspace RBAC, invitation token handling, ownership transfer, archive, project/board ancestry and roles, column version conflicts and ordering, task RBAC/rank/cursor/version/assignment/labels/copy/delete, and safe redirect continuation. Live Supabase and Google OAuth E2E requires external configuration and remains untested.

## 29. CI/CD

GitHub Actions checks each non-merge commit for `process.md`, then installs dependencies, runs format, lint, typecheck, unit and integration tests, and builds. Postgres and Redis service containers support integration. Deployment and production release gates arrive in Phase 15.

## 30. Environment Variables

`DATABASE_URL` PostgreSQL connection; `REDIS_URL` Redis connection; `API_PORT` API listener; `WEB_ORIGIN` allowed browser origin; `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` API base used by Next.js; `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` browser project URL; `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` browser-safe key; `SUPABASE_URL` exact trusted API project URL. `STORAGE_BUCKET` is reserved for Phase 12. `.env.example` contains names and local sample values, never live secrets.

## 31. Local Development

Node 20+, pnpm 10+, Docker. `pnpm install`, `docker compose up -d`, `pnpm db:migrate`, `pnpm dev`. Web on 3000 and API on 3001. `pnpm check`, `pnpm test:integration`, `pnpm build` validate the foundation. No seed command exists until domain data models are added.

## 32. Deployment Architecture

Planned: separate web and API services, managed PostgreSQL/Redis, private object storage. Terminate TLS at ingress; isolate DB/Redis networks; use managed secret injection, migrations before API rollout, rollback procedures, and backups. No production deployment exists yet. For multiple Socket.IO instances, document transport/sticky-session settings and shared Redis adapter in Phase 16.

## 33. Database Migration Rules

Check in schema and migration SQL. Generate migration locally, review destructive changes, test on a fresh DB, apply via `pnpm db:migrate` in CI/deployment. Never use schema push as a substitute for production migrations. Document data backfills and rollback plans.

## 34. Git Workflow

Focused branches and conventional commit subjects. Review staged changes, run relevant tests, update `process.md`, update this file if architecture changed, then stage/commit. Husky pre-commit and CI verify documentation. Merge commits may be exempt from per-commit process checks.

## 35. process.md Rules

Keep Current Status accurate. Every meaningful commit adds a dated `PROC-XXX` entry with summary, reason, affected modules, DB/API/socket/security effects, tests actually run, issues, decisions, current state, and next task. Never label planned functionality implemented.

## 36. Definition of Done

Implementation, strict typing, validation, server authorization, errors, relevant tests passing, usable loading/error/empty UI, real-time reconciliation where relevant, docs and process updated, and security review. A phase is complete only when its acceptance criteria run successfully.

## 37. Current Development Phase

Phases 0–10, Phase 11 inbox/core notification events/due reminders, Phase 13 task search, and part of Phase 14 security hardening are locally implemented and tested using mock JWKS. Phase 12 private attachments and Phase 11 invitation email delivery remain open. Live Supabase/Google flows need project configuration. GitHub Actions checks failed at pnpm setup because the workflow requested version 10 while `package.json` pins 10.17.1; the workflow now uses the manifest pin, and a passing remote run must be verified. See `process.md` for exact results.

## 38. Complete Product Roadmap

Phase 0 documentation/governance; 1 monorepo/web/API/Postgres/Redis/health/env/logging; 2 Supabase auth and profile; 3 workspaces/membership/RBAC/invitations; 4 projects/boards/columns; 5 tasks, assignment, rank, drag/drop; 6 authenticated Socket.IO events; 7 multi-tab Redis presence; 8 optimistic UI and version conflicts; 9 comments, mentions, typing; 10 activity history; 11 notifications; 12 attachments; 13 scoped search; 14 security/performance/accessibility hardening; 15 deployment and operations; 16 multi-instance Socket.IO Redis adapter; 17 optional Yjs collaborative notes; 18 privacy-conscious aggregate analytics. MVP requires two users to create accounts, join a workspace, use the same board, create/move/assign/comment on tasks, see live updates/presence/activity, with RBAC, secure sockets, concurrency, tests, CI, and docs.

## 39. Known Issues / Technical Debt

Files are not implemented. Search currently covers task title and description within one workspace and caps results at ten pages; project/board/comment/file content is not searched. Notification inbox reads need page refresh; due reminders are delivered to the inbox only and a scan can be delayed by up to 15 minutes. Activity writes are best effort after primary mutations, so a failed write can leave a gap; an outbox or transactional write path is needed for stronger guarantees. Authenticated project/board/task/notification/search screens and drag/drop have not been visually checked against a live signed-in session. The board view currently caps its progressive task load at 1,000 tasks and task comments/activity load at most 500 entries each across ten pages; notifications load at most 500 entries. Workspace invitation delivery is a one-time share link; email sending is not implemented. Workspace archive has no restore UI/API yet. The DB probe is a temporary infrastructure aid. Supabase credentials and Google OAuth provider are absent, so live browser authentication is untested. GitHub CI's pnpm setup mismatch has been fixed in source but a passing remote run remains to be verified; no production deployment exists. Socket publication is best-effort and cannot replay missed events; multi-instance delivery requires the planned Redis adapter. Status is updated in `process.md` after checks.

## 40. Architecture Decision Log

### ADR-001 — NestJS backend
Status: Accepted. Context: modular API with guards and socket gateway needed. Decision: NestJS. Alternatives Considered: Express/Fastify alone. Consequences: conventions and dependency injection aid maintainability; more framework setup.

### ADR-002 — PostgreSQL and Prisma
Status: Accepted. Context: relational collaborative data and migrations. Decision: PostgreSQL plus Prisma with checked-in migrations. Alternatives Considered: document store, raw SQL. Consequences: transactional constraints and type-safe access; ORM version must be pinned and migrations reviewed.

### ADR-003 — Socket.IO and Redis
Status: Socket.IO implemented in Phase 6 and Redis presence in Phase 7; Redis adapter planned. Context: authorized rooms, reconnection, multiple API instances. Decision: Socket.IO with REST refresh after small invalidation events and Redis leases for presence; add a Redis adapter in Phase 16. Alternatives Considered: raw WebSocket and database polling. Consequences: extra infrastructure and protocol versioning; REST remains authoritative. Best-effort publication has no durable replay.

### ADR-004 — Task ranking and versioning
Status: Accepted for Phase 5/8 implementation. Context: moves and conflicting edits. Decision: lexicographic ranks plus compare-and-swap integer versions. Alternatives Considered: renumbering all positions, last-write-wins. Consequences: occasional rebalance transaction; explicit conflict UI.

### ADR-005 — Supabase Auth
Status: Accepted for Phase 2 implementation. Context: email/password and Google OAuth. Decision: Supabase-hosted identity with independent API token verification. Alternatives Considered: custom password store. Consequences: provider configuration and external service dependency.

## 41. Future Features

Yjs-backed collaborative notes, live cursors, shared selection, and aggregate project analytics follow a stable MVP. Avoid invasive individual productivity tracking.

## 42. New Developer Onboarding

Clone; read `AGENTS.md`, this file, and `process.md`; inspect status/tests; install pnpm dependencies; start Docker services; migrate; run dev/check. The current feature set and next task are always in `process.md`.

## 43. AI Coding Agent Instructions

Follow the same onboarding and commit process as humans. Inspect code and tests before edits. Distinguish plan from implementation. Resolve documentation drift. Preserve security boundaries and typed public contracts. Report real test outcomes and unresolved limits.
